## 1. What Was Tested

Five ways of enforcing the **same** protocol on the **same** 100 test cases across 5
models (2,500 executions, 7 categories): (A) prose, (B) checklist, (C) self-reflection,
(D) executor + LLM-validator, and (E) the BEORYS™ external fail-closed gate. Compliance
was measured on the first output by a single deterministic verifier.

## 2. Why It Matters

If reliability came from fluency or from instructions written in prose, the prose
group would be enough. The benchmark tests whether protocol compliance during fluent
generation depends instead on **external, mandatory verification**.

## 3. Main Result

Raw first-output compliance **converged** across all groups (92%–95%): fluent text
tends to read as compliant regardless of the enforcement strategy. The external gate
(E) separated on the other metrics — highest trigger activation (93% vs. 2% for prose),
lowest unauthorized output (2%), highest auditability (0.83 vs. 0.60), and it avoided
the false-pass mode exhibited by the LLM-validator (D). Full figures are in
[`BEORYS_PCT_v0.2_RESULTS_TABLE.md`](./BEORYS_PCT_v0.2_RESULTS_TABLE.md).

## 4. Interpretation

The decisive difference is not in the wording but in **authorization**. The results are
consistent with the hypothesis that reliability comes from external, deterministic,
fail-closed verification — **under the measured conditions**, and not as universal
proof.

## 5. What Was Not Published

No prompts, no dataset, no gate implementation, no internal scripts, no blocking
criteria, no proprietary heuristics and no complete operational flow. The publication
of this repository does not constitute disclosure of the implementation.

## 6. Public Claim Supported

> The results are consistent with the hypothesis that protocol compliance failures are
> not only failures of rule knowledge, but failures of mandatory verification execution
> during fluent generation.

